I'd like to add my 0.05c to this debate (we don't have 0.02c coins in New
Zealand anymore).

It seems to me that there are two distinct ways of playing a game. You can
either "play to discover long term knowledge" or "play to win this game". To
clarify: Suppose you have a scroll of identify. You could identify (for
example) that potion, which you never really knew what it did, except giving
you a mysterious message, or you could identify (for example) a ring. If you
are playing to win, the latter is usually the choice. However if you do this,
you usually gain no "long term knowledge". I often come across this situation,
and I'm always torn by the choice. Playing for long term is more boring at the
time (and we all know that THIS is the game that's going to win, right? :-)

I played Rogue and NH3.0b7 for 4 years before I discovered rec.games.hack,
THEN I learnt a lot more about the game. I admit it might have been another 3
years before I found out as much as I know now, but I think it would have been
possible, even more so if I had "played for long term knowledge".



